# VendorRisk Triage

Next.js 16, React 19, and strict TypeScript. Sample packs live in `data/vendors/` (Northwind Logistics, Apex BioLabs, Sterling Fintech). The public app is https://vendor-risk-triage-zerocool0388.vercel.app and runs without provider keys.

## Synthetic data only

Synthetic data only. Never add real company, customer, vendor, or personal data. No employer names or work data of any kind. Sample documents stay fictional, start with "SYNTHETIC DEMO DATA. Fictional company. Not real.", and use `.example` addresses. Rebuild packs with `npm run data:generate`.

## No secrets in code

No secrets in code. Do not commit real keys. `.env.example` holds placeholders only (`OPENAI_API_KEY`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `LLM_PROVIDER`, `LLM_MODEL`). Never use a `NEXT_PUBLIC_` key. Keep `.env*` gitignored. GitHub push protection is on.

## Tests before a commit or pull request

These must pass before any commit or pull request. They are the checks in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`:

- `npm run lint` — `eslint . --max-warnings=0`
- `npm run typecheck` — `next typegen && tsc --noEmit`
- `npm run test` — Vitest (`vitest run`)
- `npm run build` — Next.js production build
- `npm run test:e2e` — Playwright (`playwright test`). Install the browser with `npx playwright install --with-deps chromium` when it is missing.

## README

The README follows this order: problem, who it's for, demo GIF, live demo link (https://vendor-risk-triage-zerocool0388.vercel.app), architecture diagram, eval results, cost and latency, limits and next steps. Only real measured numbers. Never invent metrics, eval scores, cost, or latency.

## Demo mode

Demo mode only. No paid AI calls. Never set provider keys on the deployed project. Production never makes live model calls. Evals use built-in or recorded outputs only.
